                "text": "**Analyzing the Pattern**\n\nI've made a breakthrough! I think the initial output is being sorted, row by row. Furthermore, it seems that sorting occurs according to the frequency of colors across the entire grid. I also see that the output is a 10x10 grid. I'm noting the color frequencies to confirm my hypothesis.\n\n\n**Confirming the Arrangement**\n\nI've got it! The output grid is formed by sorting all input pixels by their overall frequency, descending. Then, those sorted pixels are distributed row by row. This is how the order of the pixels becomes, as it were, \"scrambled\". The output is merely a sorted version of the input data, but split into rows and columns in the output grid.\n\n\n**Investigating the Column Data**\n\nI'm now diving into the column-wise data for each color. It seems the output is *not* built row by row. Instead, the grid is being filled from the bottom and up. It seems I am on to something with this vertical filling, so I will now focus on that aspect of the output grid. I am now counting up all the 8s, 3s, 1s, etc.\n\n\n**Determining the Distribution**\n\nI've made a key discovery. It looks like the output grid's vertical color bands are arranged in a specific way. It is now clear that the pixel distribution is controlled by the count of each color. I'm seeing that a color's count dictates how many full columns are filled, and then the remainder is placed at the bottom of the next column. This now allows me to precisely predict how each color will be displayed.\n\n\n**Refining the Column Mapping**\n\nI've got a clearer picture of the column distribution now. Colors fill columns from left to right, but there's an important detail: a new color begins filling a column at the top *before* the previous color reaches the bottom, essentially splitting columns! This is observable in train 2. I will now predict distribution with this new understanding.\n\n\n**Validating the Column Rule**\n\nI've nailed the column filling